<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Indigenous Heritage – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin-top: 10px; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Indigenous Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Researching Our History</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate both fiction and non-fiction stories. Interactive: Conduct interviews on personal interests.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a story about an Indigenous festival in Panama's comarcas, including sensory details (e.g., "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we saw woven chaquiras, heard traditional flute music, and watched the dancers perform their ancestral steps." "During the Guna Yala celebration, we admired the vibrant molas with intricate patterns and listened to chants while the community shared stories of their ancestors." "At the Emberá cultural festival, we saw beautifully painted jaguá designs on the dancers' skin, heard the rhythmic beat of the drums, and tasted freshly cooked potatoes.").
            Can conduct a brief interview about a peer's cultural background (e.g., "What traditions does your family celebrate?").
            Can describe the history of an indigenous group in class discussions (e.g., "I'm interested in learning about the Guna people's history").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of Indigenous artifacts, traditional clothing (e.g., molas, chaquiras), and landscapes from Panama's comarcas. Students are asked to verbally identify what they see and share any prior knowledge or associations in pairs. For example, "I see a <b>colorful mola</b>. It has many <b>patterns</b>." or "These are <b>beadwork</b> from the <b>Ngäbe-Buglé</b> people." This activates vocabulary related to Indigenous cultures and sets the context for speaking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing one of the displayed items or a brief experience, incorporating target vocabulary, adjectives, and simple present/past tense. "Last year, I visited a Guna Yala community. I saw <b>vibrant molas</b> with intricate <b>patterns</b>. The people there <b>preserve</b> their ancient <b>traditions</b>." The teacher explicitly models the stress in words like "Indigenous," "ancestry," "migration," "identity," and "vibrant."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a culture is 'preserved,' does it mean people forgot about it or kept it alive?" (Expected answer: Kept it alive.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are 'vibrant' colors dull or bright?" (Expected answer: Bright.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short audio recording (or reads aloud) of a narrative excerpt about a Guna Yala festival. The narrative includes sensory details and uses present simple, past simple, present perfect, and descriptive adjectives. (e.g., "The Guna people <b>have preserved</b> their <b>rich cultural heritage</b> for centuries. Last month, my family and I <b>celebrated</b> the Inna Suit festival. We <b>admired</b> the <b>vibrant molas</b> with intricate <b>patterns</b> and <b>listened</b> to traditional chants. We also <b>engaged</b> in community sharing, hearing stories of their <b>ancestors</b>. It was a truly <b>historical</b> event.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interact orally to exchange information about cultural heritage and historical importance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Oral questions):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What Indigenous group was the story about?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What festival did the narrator mention?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did they see and hear during the festival?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using present simple, past simple, and present perfect to describe Indigenous cultures and historical events; employing descriptive adjectives and target vocabulary (Indigenous, cultures, traditions, patterns, diversity, migration, heritage, preservation, history, customs, ancestry, vibrant, colorful, preserved, traditional, diverse, ancestral, cultural, historical).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Builders):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a handout related to Indigenous facts/festivals, e.g., "The Emberá people _____ (live) in _____ (traditional) villages." or "Many communities _____ (preserve) their _____ (ancestral) <b>language</b>." Students verbally complete the sentences using appropriate verbs (present/past simple, present perfect) and adjectives/nouns from the vocabulary list, focusing on correct pronunciation and stress. (e.g., "The Emberá people *live* in *traditional* villages.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Picture Description Chain):</b> The teacher displays a sequence of 3-4 pictures showing different aspects of Panamanian Indigenous culture (e.g., a Ngäbe-Buglé dance, Guna Yala molas, Emberá jaguá painting). In small groups, students take turns verbally describing one picture using at least two descriptive adjectives and one past or present simple verb, building on the previous description. "First, I see <b>colorful beadwork</b>. It <b>represents</b> their <b>identity</b>." "Then, I see people dancing. They <b>wear traditional clothing</b> and their steps <b>reflect</b> their <b>ancestry</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Q&A):</b> Students interview their partner using guided questions provided by the teacher, focusing on specific vocabulary and grammar points. Example questions: "What <b>traditional customs</b> does your family <b>celebrate</b>?" "Have you ever learned about the <b>history</b> of any <b>Indigenous</b> group in Panama?" "In your opinion, why is <b>cultural preservation</b> important?" They check if their partner used words like 'heritage', 'traditions', 'preserve'. (Pronunciation focus: minimal pairs like /ʌ/ in "customs" and /uː/ in "group").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Indigenous Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Cultural Heritage Interview and Narration"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs (A and B).
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1 (Interview):</b> Student A interviews Student B about their family's "cultural background" or a hypothetical experience at an "Indigenous festival" in Panama. Student A uses questions like: "What <b>traditions</b> does your family <b>celebrate</b>?" "Can you describe a <b>vibrant</b> festival you have heard about?" "How do you think we can <b>preserve</b> our <b>cultural heritage</b>?" Student B responds, narrating a story or describing traditions using sensory details and target vocabulary/grammar. (e.g., "At the Ngäbe-Buglé celebration, we <b>saw woven chaquiras</b>, <b>heard traditional flute music</b>, and <b>watched</b> the dancers perform their <b>ancestral steps</b>."). They practice acknowledging cultural value: "The Guna people's <b>customs are unique and valuable</b> to our nation."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2 (Narration):</b> After the interview, students swap roles. Then, each student takes turns narrating to another pair (or a small group of 4) about what they learned from their partner's interview. They narrate the information they gathered about their peer's cultural background or their described festival experience. (e.g., "My partner told me about the Emberá cultural festival. They said they <b>saw beautifully painted jaguá designs</b> on the dancers' skin, <b>heard the rhythmic beat</b> of the drums, and even <b>tasted freshly cooked potatoes</b>. They believe it's important to *sustain* these traditions.") They also share historical importance: "Groups like the Emberá <b>have lived here for centuries</b> and <b>maintain traditional ways</b>."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, natural interaction, coherent narration, effective use of present/past simple and present perfect for storytelling, incorporating descriptive adjectives and target vocabulary, and demonstrating pragmatic (acknowledging cultural value, sharing historical importance) and sociolinguistic (discussing cultural preservation) functions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking output during the "Cultural Heritage Interview and Narration" activity. Peer assessment can also be incorporated where students use a simple checklist to evaluate their partner's use of vocabulary and clarity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Cultural Heritage Interview and Narration" task from Stage 4. The teacher circulates, listening to pairs and small groups, noting student performance based on the criteria below.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, correct stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "Indigenous," "ancestry," "migration," "identity," "vibrant," "handmade").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., Indigenous, cultures, traditions, patterns, diversity, heritage, preserve, vibrant, ancestral).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Appropriate use of present simple, past simple, and present perfect for narration and description. Effective use of adjectives for detail.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Narration is understandable, interview questions are clear, and responses are relevant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction and collaboration:</b> Actively listens, asks follow-up questions, responds appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic competence:</b> Acknowledges cultural value and shares historical importance (e.g., "The Guna people's customs are unique and valuable to our nation.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word I used today was ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I was able to ask/answer questions about ______ today."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about narrating a story or conducting an interview in English today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I improve my speaking skills, especially when talking about cultural topics?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The 'Cultural Heritage Interview and Narration' activity successfully engaged most students in extended speaking. They actively used present and past simple tenses, and some adjectives. However, consistent use of the present perfect and more complex sensory details in narration needs further scaffolding."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'preservation' and 'diversity' was still a challenge for some. I need to integrate more explicit pronunciation drills."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Adjustments needed: Provide a sentence frame bank with various adjectives and present/past/present perfect verbs for narration and interview responses in the next speaking activity to support less confident speakers."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will consolidate the vocabulary and grammar learned today by writing short descriptive paragraphs about Indigenous cultures or personal cultural traditions, moving from spoken narration to written composition.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>